# Glowcap — a clump of three, one leader and two buttons.
#
# The block is an `R_CROSS` and its tile (`TextureGen.mushroom`) draws *three*
# stalks, so the model is three. That is the flower clump's reasoning and it
# applies harder here: a lone mushroom on a stalk is a nail, and what says
# "fungus" at any size is a group of them at different stages out of one patch.
#
# **The overhang is the model.** Everything else could be wrong and it would
# still read as a mushroom if the cap sticks out past the stalk by a mile, so
# the hood is 0.74 wide over a 0.115 stalk — six and a half times — and the
# widest ring is at 0.22 of the hood's own length, i.e. low, near the rim. A cap
# whose widest point is halfway up is a berry on a stick. The `assert` on that
# ratio is the one number in the checks that is not negotiable.
#
# The gill face is a real surface, not a painted disc: `cap start=flat` on a
# hood whose first ring is 0.34 wide gives an actual underside you can stand
# beneath and look up at, and the band from 0.00 to 0.22 wraps that disc round
# and *up* over the lower lip of the rim. That lip is why the glow is visible in
# profile and not only from below — at ankle height in the world, straight-up is
# the one angle nobody has.
#
# Two things the language fought over, both inherited from the flowers:
#
#  - `on=` snaps to the **closest** point on the named surface. The hood mounts
#    at `stalk` tail with `on=stem`, which is the top of the stem and therefore
#    correct; the skirt deliberately has **no** `on=`, because it mounts at 0.60
#    *up the side* of the stem and the closest surface point from there is the
#    stem's flank — the ruff would have grown sideways out of the stalk.
#  - a bone and a part may not share a name and the bone wins the `checks`
#    lookup, resolving to its *head*. Hence bone `stalk` / part `stem`, and
#    bones `bud` / parts `budstem` + `button`.
#
# Colour comes from the two places the block already defines itself. The purples
# are `TextureGen.mushroom`'s own — #9260c4 and #ce9ef0 are the two it mixes
# between, and #e0d6c6 is its stalk — so the item and the tile are the same
# plant. `Gill` is not from the tile: it is the block's own `lightColor`
# ([0.6, 0.85, 0.7] = #99d9b2) lifted a couple of steps so it reads as a source
# rather than a surface. The tile has no colour for the underside because a
# billboard has no underside, and inventing a purple one would have put the
# model at odds with the green light the block actually casts.

model mushroom
  height 0.42
  style chunky

palette
  Cap   #9260c4
  Crown #ce9ef0
  Gill  #b6efd0
  Stem  #e0d6c6

skeleton
  root soil at 0.0
  bone stalk parent=soil dir=up pitch=-3 len=0.72
  mirror
    # Buttons come out of the same soil rather than off the leader's stalk —
    # mushrooms are separate fruiting bodies, and branching them made the clump
    # read as one plant with limbs. `yaw` as well as `tilt` for the flower's
    # reason: a mirrored pair reflects across X=0 and keeps its z, so tilt alone
    # leaves all three bodies coplanar and the clump vanishes edge-on.
    #
    # `tilt` is **negative** to lean outward. On an `up` bone +tilt tips it
    # toward −X, i.e. back over the leader: written as +22 the two buttons
    # crossed under the hood and their reach measured 0.12 against a 0.20 head
    # offset — the bones travelled inward further than they were placed out.
    # `up=` lifts the head just enough that the tilted start cap does not cut
    # through y=0; without it the lint reports the clump 0.026 underground.
    bone bud parent=soil side=0.16 up=0.018 dir=up tilt=-17 pitch=5 yaw=62 len=0.34
  end

parts
  # Straight-sided and barely tapered: a mushroom stalk is a column, and the
  # conical stem the flowers use made this one look like a sapling. The flare at
  # the foot is the mycelium base and is also what the model stands on when it
  # is lying on the ground as a drop.
  loft stem bones=soil..stalk material=Stem sides=6
    ring 0.00 w=0.21 d=0.20
    ring 0.10 w=0.125 d=0.120
    ring 0.55 w=0.112 d=0.108
    ring 0.90 w=0.120 d=0.115
    ring 1.00 w=0.115 d=0.110
    cap start=flat end=flat

  # The veil ruff. Cheap (six sides, three bands) and it is the whole difference
  # between a stalk and a peg in silhouette — the one horizontal break in an
  # otherwise unbroken vertical.
  loft skirt bone=stalk at=0.60 dir=up len=0.09 material=Crown sides=6
    ring 0.00 w=0.13 d=0.13
    ring 0.30 w=0.34 d=0.33
    ring 0.72 w=0.26 d=0.25
    ring 1.00 w=0.11 d=0.11
    cap start=flat end=flat

  # Ten sides, where the flowers' heads take six or eight: this is the one part
  # of the model whose outline is a circle seen from below and from above both,
  # and at eight the rim was a visible octagon at icon size. No `roll=` and no
  # `squarish` either — those exist to break a smooth spheroid into petals, and
  # a cap is *supposed* to be a smooth dome. The read comes from the overhang.
  loft hood bone=stalk at=1.0 dir=up len=0.30 on=stem material=Cap sides=10
    ring 0.00 w=0.34 d=0.34 material=Gill   # the gill face, deep under the rim
    ring 0.14 w=0.66 d=0.66
    ring 0.22 w=0.74 d=0.74 material=Cap    # the rim — the widest thing here
    ring 0.58 w=0.64 d=0.64
    ring 0.86 w=0.42 d=0.42 material=Crown
    ring 1.00 w=0.16 d=0.16
    cap start=flat end=flat

  mirror
    loft budstem bones=bud..bud material=Stem sides=5
      ring 0.00 w=0.14 d=0.135
      ring 0.16 w=0.085 d=0.082
      ring 1.00 w=0.078 d=0.075
      cap start=flat end=flat

    # A button is the cap before the veil tears: a closed knob with the rim
    # still tucked in against the stalk. Giving it the leader's overhang would
    # have made three of the same mushroom at three sizes, which is a size
    # gradient and not a life cycle — and the flat wide hood only means
    # something if there is something un-flared next to it.
    loft button bone=bud at=1.0 dir=up pitch=-5 len=0.22 on=budstem material=Cap sides=8
      ring 0.00 w=0.16 d=0.16 material=Gill
      ring 0.16 w=0.28 d=0.28 material=Cap
      ring 0.52 w=0.32 d=0.32
      ring 0.84 w=0.24 d=0.23
      ring 1.00 w=0.09 d=0.09
      cap start=flat end=flat
  end

checks
  assert bottom(stem) < 0.01
  # The overhang, stated as the thing the whole model is for. If this ever fails
  # the cap has been narrowed to fit something and the block is a nail again.
  #
  # 3.5 and not the 6.5 the shaft is actually narrower by: `width(stem)` is a
  # bounding box, so it measures the flared foot, which is the widest part of
  # the stem by a factor of nearly two. There is no expression for "the part's
  # width up there", so the bound is set against what is measurable and the real
  # margin is comfortably larger than the number says.
  assert width(hood) > width(stem) * 3.5
  # ...and the cap is a plate, not a ball: wider than it is tall by a good
  # margin, which is what stops the dome creeping down over the stalk.
  assert width(hood) > height(hood) * 1.8
  # A leader with buttons under it, same hierarchy as the flower clumps.
  assert bottom(hood) > top(button.l)
  assert width(hood) > width(button.l) * 1.8
  # The buttons break the leader's outline rather than hiding beneath it.
  # Horizontal reach and not `abs(x)`: they are yawed off the mirror plane on
  # purpose, so half of the offset is in z (see the skeleton).
  assert (x(button.l) * x(button.l) + z(button.l) * z(button.l)) ** 0.5 > width(stem)
  # The ruff clears the stalk it rides on, or it is not visible at all.
  assert width(skirt) > width(stem) * 1.5
  assert top(skirt) < bottom(hood)
  # Y stays the long axis. A clump this wide is exactly the daisy's problem:
  # `render/ItemModels.js` normalises on the Y extent, so a squat glowcap comes
  # out of the fist and the toolbar slot bigger than everything beside it.
  assert height / width in 1.1..1.9
  assert height / depth in 1.1..1.9
  # Reported, not policed: how far off the mirror plane the buttons actually
  # sit. It is the number the `yaw` in the skeleton exists to move, and it is
  # small and hard-won — `yaw` only turns the *tilt* component of an `up` bone,
  # so it takes 62° there to buy 0.08 here.
  measure buttonz z(button.l)
  assert parts == 7
  assert tris < 700
